Do I really have to be the first to suggest Healers for beginners? IMO
they clearly have the easiest early game. Just so many unique early-
game advantages:

- They start out knowing healing (extra healing as a spell kind of
sucks) which effectively nearly doubles their longevity in combat
right off;

- Plus they get a bunch of potions that can either be taken right
away, held on to for alchemy (granted most beginners won't know about
alchemy), or used to "parry" (for a first level character, the potion
is likely to recover all HP and also increase the limit).

- And intrinsic poison resistance, easily the most vital resistance,
especially in the early game. Maybe even the next most vital
*intrinsic* after speed.

- And on top of that, stone to flesh adds extra insurance against
starvation, which is one of those things beginners have to worry about
a lot more than skilled players. (The starting apples are not great
nutrition-wise, but they can also be thrown in a pinch.

- And then above all *that*, a guaranteed starting wand of sleep,
which actually makes it reasonable to drink from fountains early and
try for that wish. Even water demons are not 100% resistant, after
all. Thus, even a largish stream of water moccasins can be dealt with
if "the fountain dries up!" and gives you a chance to beg that
Sindarin Lady's good favour so that you can zap them all and take them
out individually. This gives you the chance to put on the first few
levels quickly, unless of course you're going for the protection
racket instead...

- Oh, let's not forget that if you do go for the protection racket,
you already have the gold you need for it.

- Aaaaaaand that most wicked item of starting gear, the stethoscope -
the only other tool besides magic markers worth wishing for. Avoids
wasting time in the early game searching (and allowing not-so-pleasant
randomly generated monsters to accumulate), and greatly reduces the
agony about deciding whether to take one last swing or try to flee
somehow. Also lets you check how your pet's doing.

Sure, the Staff sucks as a Quest artifact (and the guy guarding it is
tough, and fast, and starts out wielding the Staff - of course, I
would never attempt any Quest without a wand of death, TBH), but by
that point nothing really matters any more anyway - getting a good
weapon for Gehennom is more of a luxury than anything else.

